{"nodes": [{"id": "architecture_trade_off_matrix", "label": "Trade-off matrix", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_breaks_down_when", "label": "\"Breaks down when\u2026\" column", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "The column that matters: senior interviews are mostly about knowing the limits of your own choices, and anyone can recite what they picked.", "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_scaling_by_layer", "label": "Scaling by layer (code, instances, data)", "file_type": "concept", "rationale": "Layer 1 code: async all the way down, don't lock the hot path \u2014 one .Result anywhere measured 979 \u2192 35 RPS, p99 105 ms \u2192 3531 ms on identical hardware. Layer 2 instances: stateless services behind a load balancer, broken by anything in process memory including SignalR groups. Layer 3 data: shard hot rows, queue writes, read replicas, idempotency \u2014 one hot row serialises regardless of replica count, and replicas triple DB load.", "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_five_sentences", "label": "The five sentences worth memorising", "file_type": "concept", "rationale": "1) A race condition is the gap between read and write, not simultaneity. 2) async is for waiting, Parallel is for working, Task.Run is neither. 3) Optimistic pays in wasted work, pessimistic pays in waiting, contention decides. 4) How you aggregate matters more than whether you parallelise. 5) Kubernetes multiplies instances; it doesn't make un-scalable code scalable.", "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_idempotency_key", "label": "Idempotency key", "file_type": "concept", "rationale": "Gains: retries stop duplicating money. Costs: a key per operation, storage, an expiry policy. Breaks down when the client doesn't send a stable key \u2014 without one, 5 retries charged 5\u00d7.", "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_one_atomic_statement", "label": "One atomic statement (SET balance = balance - n WHERE \u2026)", "file_type": "concept", "rationale": "No coordination needed at all, but only works if the logic fits in one statement \u2014 it breaks down when your decision needs data the statement cannot see.", "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_throttled_fanout", "label": "Throttled fan-out vs unbounded Task.WhenAll", "file_type": "concept", "rationale": "Task.WhenAll: 2005 ms \u2192 204 ms on 5 threads, but unbounded fan-out means with 10,000 items you are your dependency's outage. Throttling with SemaphoreSlim protects the dependency and is slower on purpose (603 ms vs 204 ms); it breaks down when Release() isn't in a finally and the pool shrinks to nothing.", "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_valuetask", "label": "ValueTask on hot sync paths", "file_type": "concept", "rationale": "Zero allocation versus 720 KB, but it can only be awaited once \u2014 await it twice or store it and it is worse than Task.", "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}], "edges": [{"source": "architecture_trade_off_matrix", "target": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_breaks_down_when", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "architecture_trade_off_matrix", "target": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_scaling_by_layer", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "architecture_trade_off_matrix", "target": "architecture_trade_off_matrix_five_sentences", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "architecture_trade_off_matrix", "target": "adr_0003_target_dotnet_8_lts", "relation": "cites", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "architecture_trade_off_matrix", "target": "adr_0004_hybrid_persistence", "relation": "cites", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "architecture_trade_off_matrix", "target": "adr_0005_postgresql_over_sqlserver_sqlite", "relation": "cites", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "architecture_trade_off_matrix", "target": "adr_0007_optimistic_by_default", "relation": "cites", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/architecture/trade-off-matrix.md", "